\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Relations between the distributions that describe the quark structure of the nucleon. Integrating the generalized TMDs (or their Fourier transforms, the Wigner distributions) over transverse position gives TMDs, and integrating over transverse momentum gives GPDs. The collinear PDFs are obtained from TMDs by integrating over $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {k}}_{\mathrm T}$ and from GPDs in the forward limit. Moments of GPDs in $x$ give the elastic form factors.\relax }}{3}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Schematic picture of the Sivers effect. For a nucleon moving into the page ($\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}$ along $\hat {\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {z}}}$) with transverse spin $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {S}}_{\mathrm T}$ along $\hat {\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {y}}}$, the second term of Eq.~\eqref {eq:intro_sivers_density} becomes $-\,k_x f_{1T}^\perp /m_N$. A nonzero Sivers function therefore shifts the density of unpolarized quarks along $\hat {\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {x}}}$, perpendicular to both $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}$ and $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {S}}_{\mathrm T}$ (solid circle), relative to the symmetric unpolarized distribution (dashed). The shift shown, toward $+\hat {\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {x}}}$, corresponds to $f_{1T}^\perp <0$.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Handbag diagram for the leading contribution to the DIS hadronic tensor. The virtual photon scatters off a quark of momentum $k$ with $k^+ = xP^+$, and the lower blob is the quark-quark correlator whose $\gamma ^+$ projection gives the PDF $f_1(x)$. The dashed line indicates the sum over final states.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Staple-shaped gauge links connecting the quark fields at $0$ and $b$ in the TMD correlator, Eq.~\eqref {eq:tmd_correlator_simple}. (a) In SIDIS the link runs to $+\infty $ along the light-cone direction $\ensuremath {n}$ of the outgoing quark, Eq.~\eqref {eq:staple_sidis}. (b) In Drell-Yan it runs to $-\infty $, Eq.~\eqref {eq:staple_dy}. The dashed segment is the transverse link at infinity. In the lattice calculation the legs have finite length $\eta |v|$ along a space-like direction $v$, and the two cases correspond to $\eta v{\cdot }P\to +\infty $ and $\eta v{\cdot }P\to -\infty $ \cite {Musch:2011er}.\relax }}{23}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Quark fields $\psi (x)$ and $\psi (x+a\hat \mu )$ on the sites of a cubic spacetime lattice, the link variable $U_\mu (x)$ between them, and the plaquette $P_{\mu \nu }(y)$ of Eq.~\eqref {eq:plaquette}. The product $U_\mu (x)\psi (x+a\hat \mu )$ transforms in the same way as $\psi (x)$. Reproduced from Ref.~\cite {Lin:2014}, Fig.~1.1.\relax }}{34}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Staple-shaped gauge link $\ensuremath {{\mathcal {U}}[0, \eta v, \eta v + b, b]}$. The staple points along the direction $v$, in general $v=(0,v_1,v_2,v_3)$, and the limit $\eta \to \infty $ extends it.\relax }}{49}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Directions of the spatial staple vectors $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}$ mapped in the $v_1-v_3$ plane. Each vector corresponds to the requisite geometry to satisfy the $b \cdot P \neq 0$ constraint for the four sequential Collins-Soper parameters $\hat {\zeta }$.\relax }}{63}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Ideal continuous trajectory of the off-axis staple vector (solid blue) for $P_1 = -1$. Circular markers indicate target continuous coordinates for sequential values of $\eta $. Dashed lines illustrate the discrete, step-wise permutations of gauge links used to reach the surrounding bounding integer lattice sites.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Discrete gauge link configurations defining the interpolation bounds for $P_1 = -1$. Empty brackets $\{\}$ denote a zero-length origin link, while numerical arrays (e.g., $\{1,3,1\}$) give the sequence of directional steps.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Ideal continuous trajectory and corresponding discrete bounding paths for the off-axis staple vector at $P_1 = -2$.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Discrete gauge link configurations defining the interpolation bounds for $P_1 = -2$.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Ideal continuous trajectory and corresponding discrete bounding paths for the off-axis staple vector at $P_1 = -3$.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Discrete gauge link configurations defining the interpolation bounds for $P_1 = -3$.\relax }}{64}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Ideal continuous trajectory and corresponding discrete bounding paths for the off-axis staple vector at $P_1 = -4$.\relax }}{65}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Discrete gauge link configurations defining the interpolation bounds for $P_1 = -4$.\relax }}{65}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Illustration of path degeneracy. Two distinct step-wise gauge link permutations (e.g., solid and dashed lines) terminate at the identical discrete lattice coordinate. We average the correlators from these equivalent paths to suppress local gauge noise before interpolation.\relax }}{65}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Quadratic interpolation of the real and imaginary parts of the correlation function for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}}=\{1,2,0\}$, $\eta =7$, and $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=(-1,0,0)$. Red markers indicate the path-averaged lattice data points as a function of their deviation from the ideal constraint trajectory, $\Delta v_1 = (v_1^{\text {constraint}} - v_1^{\text {lattice}})/a$. The gray band indicates the quadratic fit (`InterpolationOrder $\rightarrow $ 2`), and the central blue marker represents the final extracted value at $\Delta v_1 = 0$, lying exactly on the required off-axis spatial trajectory.\relax }}{67}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces The sampled operator separation vectors mapped in the spatial $b_1-b_2$ plane (where $b_1 \equiv b_L$ and $b_2 \equiv b_T$).\relax }}{68}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Mapping of the spatial lattice vectors into the physical invariant Variable Space $(b \cdot P/(2\pi ), |b|/a)$. The trajectories diverge based on the selected target $\hat {\zeta }$.\relax }}{68}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces Coverage of the quark separation vectors across the simulated lattice volume and their corresponding physical Variable Space representation.\relax }}{68}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Discrete gauge link configurations for purely transverse quark separations ($\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}} = \{0, b_2, 0\}$). Numerical arrays give the sequential lattice steps (e.g., `2` represents a shift in the $x_2$ direction).\relax }}{69}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces Gauge link sequences for $+b_1$ (positive longitudinal separation).\relax }}{70}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces Mirrored gauge link sequences for $-b_1$ (negative longitudinal separation).\relax }}{70}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces Symmetric sets of step-wise gauge link paths required to access finite $\pm b \cdot P$ and isolate the real and imaginary components of the invariant amplitudes.\relax }}{70}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces Illustration of path degeneracy for the quark separation vector $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}}$. Distinct step-wise permutations (solid and dashed lines) terminating at the same Cartesian coordinate are averaged to suppress local gauge noise.\relax }}{71}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces The full network of path-averaged $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}}$ vectors in the $b_1/a - b_2/a$ plane. Solid red lines represent the ideal vectors, while dashed lines illustrate the underlying degenerate staircase paths calculated and averaged on the discrete lattice.\relax }}{71}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27}{\ignorespaces Real and imaginary parts of the $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}$ amplitude for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-1,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space.\relax }}{82}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28}{\ignorespaces Real and imaginary parts of the $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}$ amplitude for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-1,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space.\relax }}{82}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Im}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-1,0,0\}$ at fixed $\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a=8$. The top two rows display the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a)-$space, while the bottom two rows display the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2)-$space. Notice the lack of discernible signal in $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Im}}$.\relax }}{83}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30}{\ignorespaces Real and imaginary parts of the $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}$ amplitude for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-2,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space.\relax }}{84}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31}{\ignorespaces Real and imaginary parts of the $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}$ amplitude for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-2,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space.\relax }}{84}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32}{\ignorespaces $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Im}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-2,0,0\}$ at fixed $\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a=8$. The top two rows display the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a)-$space, while the bottom two rows display the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2)-$space.\relax }}{85}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33}{\ignorespaces Real and imaginary parts of the $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}$ amplitude for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-3,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space.\relax }}{86}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34}{\ignorespaces Real and imaginary parts of the $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}$ amplitude for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-3,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space.\relax }}{86}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {35}{\ignorespaces $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Im}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-3,0,0\}$ at fixed $\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a=8$. The top two rows display the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a)-$space, while the bottom two rows display the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2)-$space.\relax }}{87}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {36}{\ignorespaces Real and imaginary parts of the $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}$ amplitude for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-4,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space.\relax }}{88}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37}{\ignorespaces Real and imaginary parts of the $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}$ amplitude for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-4,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space.\relax }}{88}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38}{\ignorespaces $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Im}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-4,0,0\}$ at fixed $\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a=8$. The top two rows display the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a)-$space, while the bottom two rows display the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2)-$space.\relax }}{89}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39}{\ignorespaces The $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$ amplitude at fixed momentum ($P=-1$) and fixed $\eta $ (here $\eta =8$). The short-distance data ($|\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}}| < 3a$) curve in the opposite direction to the long-distance data ($|\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}}| \ge 3a$).\relax }}{92}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {40}{\ignorespaces The $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ amplitude at fixed momentum ($P=-1$) and fixed $\eta $ (here $\eta =8$). The short-distance data ($|\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}}| < 3a$) curve in the opposite direction to the long-distance data ($|\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}}| \ge 3a$).\relax }}{93}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {41}{\ignorespaces Failure of Gaussian model fit for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$ (with $\alpha -\beta = -0.0427(27)$ and $\chi ^2/\text {dof}\approx 235$) and $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ (with $\alpha -\beta = -0.022(25)$ and $\chi ^2/\text {dof}\approx 1.4$) amplitudes.\relax }}{94}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {42}{\ignorespaces The top \texttt {PySR} analytical models ($C = 11, 14, 16, 17$) compared with the lattice data for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$ at fixed staple extents $\eta = 6, 7, 8, 10$ (with $P_1 = -1$ and $|\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}}| \geq 3a$). Each panel shows the amplitude as a function of the longitudinal separation $b_L/a$ at fixed transverse separations $b_T$, with the four Pareto-front models overlaid. The two exponential-$\eta $ models ($C = 14$, solid; $C = 16$, dashed) give the most balanced description of the data across all $\eta $ values, while the pure power-law-$\eta $ models ($C = 11$, dotted; $C = 17$, dot-dashed) deviate slightly and systematically at the ends of the $\eta $ range.\relax }}{99}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {43}{\ignorespaces Diagnostic straight-line test for the Gaussian hypothesis. If the amplitude followed a pure Gaussian profile $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}} \propto e^{-c\,b^2}$, the data would appear as a straight line on a logarithmic ordinate versus $b^2$ (red dashed line, best Gaussian fit with $c = 0.058$). The lattice data (black markers) curve upward away from the Gaussian line at large $b^2$, indicating a heavier-than-Gaussian tail, which the \texttt {PySR} power-law form $(1 + b^2)^{-1.39}$ (blue solid line) accurately captures. The curvature is direct, model-independent evidence that the spatial decay of the non-local quark bilinear follows an algebraic power law rather than a Gaussian exponential.\relax }}{100}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {44}{\ignorespaces 3D jackknife fits of $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}(b_L,b_T,\eta )$ to a family of 16 Gaussian ans\"atze, all built on the common baseline $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}} = a\, e^{-f\eta }\, \exp \!\big [-c\,(b_L P_L)^2 - d\,(b_L^2+b_T^2)\big ]$, in which the coefficients $c$ and $d$ are each independently allowed to be either constant or one of three $\eta $-dependent forms, $c\to c(\eta )$ and/or $d\to d(\eta )$.\relax }}{101}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {45}{\ignorespaces 3D Jackknife fits of $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}(b_L,b_T,\eta )$ to a family of power-law ans\"atze, all built on the common baseline $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}} = a\, e^{-f\eta }\, \left (1+(c+d)b^2_L+db^2_T\right )^{-j}$, in which the coefficient $c$ is either constant or one of several $\eta $-dependent forms, $c\to c(\eta )$.\relax }}{102}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {46}{\ignorespaces 3D jackknife fits of $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}(b_L,b_T,\eta )$ to a family of power-law ans\"atze, all built on the common baseline $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}} = a\, e^{-f\eta }\, \left (1+(c+d)b^2_L+db^2_T\right )^{-j}$, in which the coefficient $c$ takes further $\eta $-dependent forms, $c\to c(\eta )$, that yield wider Fourier transforms in $x$ space.\relax }}{103}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {47}{\ignorespaces Sequential Lorentzian fits for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$ at individual $\eta $ slices, holding the global power $j=2$ and amplitude $a=4$ constant.\relax }}{104}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {48}{\ignorespaces The extracted parameter $c(\eta )$ overlaid with the candidate asymptotic models $c_1(\eta ),\ldots ,c_{10}(\eta )$. The extracted values approach an asymptote at large $\eta $.\relax }}{104}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {49}{\ignorespaces The extracted parameter $d(\eta )$, which shows no visible dependence on the staple extent $\eta $.\relax }}{104}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {50}{\ignorespaces Lattice extracted parameters $c$ (left) and $d$ (right) for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ as a function of $\eta $. Both parameters show no visible dependence on $\eta $.\relax }}{106}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {51}{\ignorespaces Lattice extracted parameters $c$ (left) and $d$ (right) for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$ as a function of $\eta $. The data are noisier than for the real amplitudes, but consistent with the constant-parameter ansatz.\relax }}{106}{figure.caption.53}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {52}{\ignorespaces Fit for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-1,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space with the global simultaneous fit overlaid.\relax }}{109}{figure.caption.55}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {53}{\ignorespaces Fit for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-1,0,0\}$ at a fixed staple extent $\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a=8$. The top two rows display the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a)-$space, while the bottom two rows display the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2)-$space with the fit.\relax }}{110}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {54}{\ignorespaces $x$-dependence at $b^2=9a^2$ for $P_L=-1$. Top row: $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Re}}_{2B}$, $i\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Im}}_{2B}$, and $\tilde f_1^{(0)}=2\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]}_{2B}$. Bottom row: $\tilde f_{1T}^{\perp (1)}=-2\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Re}}_{12B}$, $\langle k_y\rangle _{TU}$, and $x\langle k_y\rangle _{TU}$. Bands show the Jackknife uncertainty.\relax }}{111}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {55}{\ignorespaces The quantities of Fig.~\ref {fig:ft_xdep_2d_P1} as functions of $x$ and $b^2/a^2\in [9,68]$ for $P_L=-1$.\relax }}{111}{figure.caption.59}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {56}{\ignorespaces Fit for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-2,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space with the global simultaneous fit overlaid.\relax }}{112}{figure.caption.61}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {57}{\ignorespaces Fit for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-2,0,0\}$ at a fixed staple extent $\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a=8$. The top two rows display the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a)-$space, while the bottom two rows display the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2)-$space with the fit.\relax }}{113}{figure.caption.62}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {58}{\ignorespaces $x$-dependence at $b^2=9a^2$ for $P_L=-2$. Top row: $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Re}}_{2B}$, $i\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Im}}_{2B}$, and $\tilde f_1^{(0)}=2\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]}_{2B}$. Bottom row: $\tilde f_{1T}^{\perp (1)}=-2\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Re}}_{12B}$, $\langle k_y\rangle _{TU}$, and $x\langle k_y\rangle _{TU}$. Bands show the Jackknife uncertainty.\relax }}{114}{figure.caption.64}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {59}{\ignorespaces The quantities of Fig.~\ref {fig:ft_xdep_2d_P2} as functions of $x$ and $b^2/a^2\in [9,68]$ for $P_L=-2$.\relax }}{115}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {60}{\ignorespaces Fit for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-3,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space with the global simultaneous fit overlaid.\relax }}{116}{figure.caption.67}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {61}{\ignorespaces Fit for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-3,0,0\}$ at a fixed staple extent $\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a=8$. The top two rows display the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a)-$space, while the bottom two rows display the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2)-$space with the fit.\relax }}{117}{figure.caption.68}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {62}{\ignorespaces $x$-dependence at $b^2=9a^2$ for $P_L=-3$. Top row: $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Re}}_{2B}$, $i\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Im}}_{2B}$, and $\tilde f_1^{(0)}=2\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]}_{2B}$. Bottom row: $\tilde f_{1T}^{\perp (1)}=-2\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Re}}_{12B}$, $\langle k_y\rangle _{TU}$, and $x\langle k_y\rangle _{TU}$. Bands show the Jackknife uncertainty.\relax }}{118}{figure.caption.70}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {63}{\ignorespaces The quantities of Fig.~\ref {fig:ft_xdep_2d_P3} as functions of $x$ and $b^2/a^2\in [9,68]$ for $P_L=-3$.\relax }}{118}{figure.caption.71}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {64}{\ignorespaces Fit for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-4,0,0\}$. The top row displays the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space, while the bottom row displays the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2,\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a)-$space with the global simultaneous fit overlaid.\relax }}{119}{figure.caption.73}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {65}{\ignorespaces Fit for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$ \& $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$ amplitudes for $\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot a\hat {L}/(2\pi )=\{-4,0,0\}$ at a fixed staple extent $\eta |\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {v}}|/a=8$. The top two rows display the $(b_{L}/a,b_{T}/a)-$space, while the bottom two rows display the $((\ensuremath {\boldsymbol {P}}\cdot \ensuremath {\boldsymbol {b}})\cdot \hat {L}/(2\pi ),b^{2}/a^2)-$space with the fit.\relax }}{120}{figure.caption.74}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {66}{\ignorespaces $x$-dependence at $b^2=9a^2$ for $P_L=-4$. Top row: $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Re}}_{2B}$, $i\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Im}}_{2B}$, and $\tilde f_1^{(0)}=2\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]}_{2B}$. Bottom row: $\tilde f_{1T}^{\perp (1)}=-2\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Re}}_{12B}$, $\langle k_y\rangle _{TU}$, and $x\langle k_y\rangle _{TU}$. Bands show the Jackknife uncertainty.\relax }}{121}{figure.caption.76}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {67}{\ignorespaces The quantities of Fig.~\ref {fig:ft_xdep_2d_P4} as functions of $x$ and $b^2/a^2\in [9,68]$ for $P_L=-4$.\relax }}{122}{figure.caption.77}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {68}{\ignorespaces Factorization ratio test at $\eta |v|/a = 8$, $P_L = -1$ ($\hat {\zeta } = 0.090772$) for $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Re}}$, $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{2B}^{\text {Im}}$ and $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}_{12B}^{\text {Re}}$. Top row: $T(P\cdot b, b^2)$ of Eq.~\eqref {eq:factorization_T}, the ratio to the $P\cdot b = 4$ row, plotted against $b^2$. Bottom row: $B(P\cdot b, b^2)$ of Eq.~\eqref {eq:factorization_B}, the ratio to $b^2 = 18$, plotted against $P\cdot b$. Under factorization, every set of points is flat. Filled circles use linear interpolation in $b^2$ on all three rows. Open triangles use quadratic interpolation on the $P\cdot b = 0$ and $4$ rows and linear interpolation on the $P\cdot b = 3$ row (Table~\ref {tab:factorization_grid}). A triangle is omitted where both schemes give the same value, which happens for every point that involves only the $P\cdot b = 3$ row and data points. Error bars are Jackknife errors and do not include any uncertainty from the interpolation.\relax }}{124}{figure.caption.80}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {69}{\ignorespaces Fits testing the separability hypothesis. Pairing an exponential decay in $(P\cdot b)$ with a Gaussian transverse profile gives a poor fit ($\chi ^2/\text {dof} \approx 4.98$). In models with a lower $\chi ^2/\text {dof}$, the coefficient $c$ consistently fits to zero within uncertainties and is negative in each Jackknife set, so the data reject the separability hypothesis.\relax }}{126}{figure.caption.81}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {70}{\ignorespaces Fits testing the separability hypothesis with $\eta $-dependent $c$, written $c(\eta )$. In models with a lower $\chi ^2/\text {dof}$, the coefficient $c$ is now positive.\relax }}{127}{figure.caption.82}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {71}{\ignorespaces Discrete $\hat \zeta $-slices of the reconstructed amplitudes, unpolarized and Sivers TMD combinations, and Sivers shift, plotted against both $x$ and $1/\hat \zeta $ at fixed transverse separation $|b_T| = 3a \approx 0.34$ fm. Each ribbon corresponds to one of the four simulated momenta $P_L = -1,-2,-3,-4$ (equivalently $1/\hat \zeta = 11.02,\ 3.40,\ 1.85,\ 1.27$). The systematic drift of every panel with $1/\hat \zeta $ motivates the extrapolation to the physical light-cone limit $\hat \zeta \to \infty $ performed below.\relax }}{132}{figure.caption.84}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {72}{\ignorespaces Extrapolation of the twelve simultaneous-fit parameters to the physical limit $\hat \zeta \to \infty $, using only the three higher momenta $P_L=-2,-3,-4$ (black points, plotted against $1/\hat \zeta $). The dashed red curve and shaded band show the fitted ansatz $c+d/\hat \zeta ^2$ of Eq.~\ref {eq:zetahat_extrap_ansatz}, with each panel's Jackknife $\chi ^2/\text {dof}$ noted in its title; the blue square at $1/\hat \zeta =0$ marks the extrapolated physical-limit value. The parameters $c_{\text {re}}$, $c_{\text {im}}$, and $c_{\text {reA12B}}$ are shown rescaled by $1/P_L^2$, and $a_{\text {im}}$ by $1/P_L$, as described in the text.\relax }}{133}{figure.caption.85}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {73}{\ignorespaces Unpolarized and Sivers TMD amplitudes reconstructed from the extrapolated ($\hat \zeta \to \infty $) fit parameters of Fig.~\ref {fig:param_extrapolation_zetahat}, evaluated as continuous functions of $x$ at fixed transverse separation $|b_T| = 3a \approx 0.34$ fm: $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Re}}_{2B}$, $i\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Im}}_{2B}$, the full unpolarized combination $\tilde f_1^{(0)}=2\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]}_{2B}$, the Sivers combination $\tilde f_{1T}^{\perp (1)}=-2\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle A$}\mathaccent "0365{A}^{[x]\text {Re}}_{12B}$, and the resulting Sivers shift $\langle k_y \rangle _{TU}$ and $x\langle k_y \rangle _{TU}$, the latter two restricted to the quark region $x\ge 0$. Shaded bands are the Jackknife uncertainty propagated from the twelve extrapolated parameters.\relax }}{134}{figure.caption.86}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {74}{\ignorespaces The same six extrapolated ($\hat \zeta \to \infty $) quantities as Fig.~\ref {fig:extrapolated_amplitude_level}, shown here as full surfaces in $x$ and transverse separation $|b_T|$ (ranging from $3a\approx 0.34$ fm to $\sqrt {68}\,a\approx 0.94$ fm). The joint $x$-$|b_T|$ dependence shows how the reconstructed amplitudes and the extracted Sivers shift change with transverse quark separation at the physical limit.\relax }}{135}{figure.caption.88}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {75}{\ignorespaces Comparison, at fixed $|b_T| = 3a \approx 0.34$ fm, between the six physical quantities evaluated at each of the three finite momenta used in the extrapolation ($P_L=-2,-3,-4$, colored curves, each using its own unextrapolated fit parameters) and the extrapolated $\hat \zeta \to \infty $ result (black dashed curve). The extrapolated curve differs from all three finite-momentum curves, including the one at the largest simulated momentum, so the fit-parameter extrapolation of Fig.~\ref {fig:param_extrapolation_zetahat} captures real momentum dependence and does not just reproduce the $P_L=-4$ result.\relax }}{136}{figure.caption.89}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {76}{\ignorespaces Left and center: the optimal separable factors $g(x)$ and $h(|b_T|)$ (Eqs.~\eqref {eq:factorization_h_solution}-\eqref {eq:factorization_g_selfconsistent}, normalized so $\text {mean}[g^2]=1$) for the extrapolated Sivers shift, shown for all four $x$-windows of Table~\ref {tab:factorization_sivers}. Right: the reconstructed $g(x)h(|b_T|)$ (dashed) compared to the actual extrapolated Sivers shift (solid) at the smallest and largest fitted $|b_T|$, using the $x\in [0,1]$ fit; the visible gap between the solid and dashed curves is the residual quantified in Table~\ref {tab:factorization_sivers}.\relax }}{139}{figure.caption.91}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {77}{\ignorespaces $R_b(x) = \langle k_y \rangle _{TU}(x,|b_T|)/\langle k_y \rangle _{TU}(x,0.34\text { fm})$ for four values of $|b_T|$, as a function of $x$. Under exact factorization each curve would be flat. Instead, every curve rises from its $x\to 0$ value to a peak near $x\approx 0.1$ before decaying, so the $|b_T|$-dependence of the Sivers shift itself depends on $x$.\relax }}{139}{figure.caption.92}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {78}{\ignorespaces $R_x(|b_T|) = \langle k_y \rangle _{TU}(x,|b_T|)/\langle k_y \rangle _{TU}(0.3,|b_T|)$ for four values of $x$, as a function of $|b_T|$. The $x=0.2$ curve changes by more than a factor of two across the fitted $|b_T|$ range instead of staying flat, so the $x$-dependence of the Sivers shift itself depends on $|b_T|$, consistent with Fig.~\ref {fig:factorization_ratio_x_sivers}.\relax }}{140}{figure.caption.93}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {79}{\ignorespaces The same ratio test as Fig.~\ref {fig:factorization_ratio_x_sivers}, $R_b(x)$, repeated at each finite momentum individually using its own raw (unextrapolated) fit parameters. None of the four panels shows a flat curve, so the non-factorization is a feature of the fitted amplitudes and not an artifact of the $\hat \zeta \to \infty $ extrapolation.\relax }}{141}{figure.caption.94}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {80}{\ignorespaces The same ratio test as Fig.~\ref {fig:factorization_ratio_bT_sivers}, $R_x(|b_T|)$, repeated at each finite momentum individually. The departure from flatness grows with $|P_L|$, most visibly in the $x=0.2$ curve, which is consistent with the parameter mismatch identified in the extrapolated $j_X$ and $d_X$ values above.\relax }}{142}{figure.caption.95}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {81}{\ignorespaces Optimal separable factor $g(x)$ for each of the four amplitude combinations of Table~\ref {tab:factorization_amplitudes}, for all four $x$-windows (normalized so $\text {mean}[g^2]=1$). Each amplitude's $g(x)$ is dominated by a sharp rise as $x\to 0$, which steepens as the window narrows and reflects the Bessel-function behavior at small $x$.\relax }}{143}{figure.caption.97}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {82}{\ignorespaces Optimal separable factor $h(|b_T|)$ for each of the four amplitude combinations of Table~\ref {tab:factorization_amplitudes}, for all four $x$-windows. For all four amplitudes, $h(|b_T|)$ falls smoothly with increasing $|b_T|$, but its magnitude and slope depend on the $x$-window used to define the fit, which itself signals imperfect factorization.\relax }}{144}{figure.caption.98}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {83}{\ignorespaces Reconstructed $g(x)h(|b_T|)$ (dashed) against the actual extrapolated amplitude data (solid) at the smallest and largest fitted $|b_T|$, using the $x\in [0,1]$ fit, for each of the four amplitude combinations of Table~\ref {tab:factorization_amplitudes}. The largest reconstruction gap appears near $x\to 0$ for every amplitude, consistent with the $x\in [0,1]$ column of Table~\ref {tab:factorization_amplitudes} having the largest relative distance in every row.\relax }}{145}{figure.caption.99}%
